---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/how-much-tip-waitress-waiter/" jsonLd={jsonLd} nav={nav}>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="guide-intro"><a href="/">Restaurant tip calculator</a> / Waiter &amp; waitress tips</p>
    <h1>How Much to Tip a Waitress or Waiter</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The short answer: <strong>15–20% of the pre-tax bill</strong> — 15% for merely adequate service, 20% for good,
      22–25% for great. Here's the full breakdown with examples.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Calculator mode="dinein" />

  <aside class="ad ad-leaderboard ad-below" aria-label="Advertisement">
    <p class="ad-label">Advertisement</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" id="ad-below" data-size="728 × 90 · 300 × 250">
      <!-- e.g. GAM slot: /XXXXXXX/rtc_waiter_below-calc -->
    </div>
  </aside>

  <!-- ============ GUIDE ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="guide">
    <h2>Tips for waiters and waitresses: how much at each level</h2>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="guide-intro">
          The scale below is what etiquette guides, servers, and diners actually agree on:
        </p>
        <div class="menu">
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Adequate, forgettable service</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"
            ></span><span class="menu-val">15%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Good, solid service</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"></span><span
              class="menu-val">18%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Great service — the norm</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"
            ></span><span class="menu-val">20%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Exceptional, went above and beyond</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"
            ></span><span class="menu-val">22–25%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Poor service (not the server's fault)</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"
            ></span><span class="menu-val">10–15%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="menu-row">
            <span class="menu-name">Large groups (6+)</span><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"></span><span
              class="menu-val">18–20% auto-added</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="menu-src">
          Should you tip on the pre-tax subtotal? <a href="/#tip-on-tax">Yes, if you want to be precise</a> — the
          difference is under a dollar on most checks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <table class="worked">
          <caption>Tip on a $60 check, by service level:</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">Tip</th><th scope="col">Total</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Adequate · 15%</td><td>$9.00</td><td>$69.00</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Good · 18%</td><td>$10.80</td><td>$70.80</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Great · 20%</td><td>$12.00</td><td>$72.00</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Exceptional · 22%</td><td>$13.20</td><td>$73.20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Best night ever · 25%</td><td>$15.00</td><td>$75.00</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="worked-note">
          Your check's different? <a href="#calculator">Run it through the calculator</a> — it updates as you type.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WHY ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="why">
    <h2>Why the tip is most of your server's pay</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        In most U.S. states, restaurants can pay servers a tipped minimum wage as low as <strong>$2.13 an hour</strong>
        (U.S. Department of Labor), with tips expected to cover the rest. That means the tip isn't a bonus — it's the
        bulk of your waiter's or waitress's income, sometimes shared with bartenders, bussers, and food runners through
        a tip pool. Tipping 20% "even when service is fine" isn't generosity; it's how the system is priced — and 20% tracks
        the <a href="/average-restaurant-tip/">full-service dining average</a> in U.S. point-of-sale data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="faq">
    <h2>Waitress and waiter tip questions, answered</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>How much should you tip a waitress or waiter?</summary>
        <p>
          Tip your waiter or waitress 15–20% of the pre-tax bill for table service: 15% for adequate but unremarkable
          service, 18% for good service, and 20–25% for great or exceptional service. Below 15% signals that something
          went wrong.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the average tip for waiters and waitresses?</summary>
        <p>
          The average tip for waiters and waitresses in the U.S. is about 20% of the bill at full-service restaurants,
          with the standard range between 15% and 20%. Industry point-of-sale data puts full-service averages just
          under 20%.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much tip do you leave on a $40 restaurant bill?</summary>
        <p>
          On a $40 bill: 15% is $6.00, 18% is $7.20, 20% is $8.00 and a 22% tip is $8.80. Most people would leave $8.00
          for good service — $48.00 total.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do you tip a waiter per drink or on the full check?</summary>
        <p>
          Tip on the full check — drinks included — or leave $1–2 per drink at the bar. If your server also makes your
          drinks and the tab is mainly alcohol, 15–20% of the whole check still works.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do you still tip when a gratuity is already added?</summary>
        <p>
          No — if an automatic gratuity of 18–20% is already on the check (common for parties of six or more), that is
          the tip. Adding a small extra for standout service is optional and always welcome. Not sure what the line
          means? See our guide to the <a href="/service-charge-on-restaurant-bill/">service charge on your restaurant
          bill</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Should you tip more than 20%?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, when service went above and beyond — attentive pacing, accommodated requests, strong recommendations,
          difficult seating or a large mobile order. 22–25% is a fair way to say thank you, and exceptional service at
          high-end restaurants commonly earns it.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <aside class="ad ad-native ad-inline" aria-label="Advertisement">
    <p class="ad-label">Advertisement</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" id="ad-native" data-size="Native banner · 280px reserved">
      <!-- Paste the native banner tag from your ad network here -->
    </div>
  </aside>

  <Related exclude="/how-much-tip-waitress-waiter/" />
</Layout>